## Introduction
The targeted design of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with chemical functionalities promise advanced applications in the fields of molecular separation1 and recognition,2,3 catalysis4–6 and drug delivery.7–9 Such applications crucially rely on selective interactions between the target molecules and the host. Depending on the MOF topology, these interactions are mediated either by the organic linkers2,10,11 or by strong coordinative interactions between Lewis acidic metal sites and Lewis basic guests.12–14 Among the latter, aromatic N-heterocyclic compounds like indole,15–17 carbazole15,16 and quinoline18 derivates with sizes comparable to the upper limit of micropores $\cdot<2\mathrm{nm})$ belong to the most environmentally harmful molecules which are, nevertheless, natural components of liquid fuels, petroleum and refinery streams.12,13,15,19 Their combustion produces $\mathsf{N O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ radicals and contributes to acid rain.13,19 Considerable effort was made on the removal of such molecules from liquids, in particular with MOFs featuring coordinatively unsaturated metal sites (CUS).15–20
Various metal-organic framework types with CUS host paramagnetic transition metal cations like ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in HKUST-1, $^{26}\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ in CPO-27,15,27 ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ in MIL- $100^{28}$ and MIL101.7,29 Here we show, that for adsorption processes involving CUS in such systems, ssNMR studies offer additional insight. While paramagnetic NMR spectroscopic studies on MOFs are still rare, the method is well developed for structure determination of biomolecules in the liquid state.30–35 Guest molecules coordinated at the CUS will experience large hyperfine shifts leading to differences of several hundreds of ppm compared to noncoordinated and diamagnetic guest molecules.36 While individual resonances might be severely broadened, large shift dispersions still result in excellent resolution. Even for guest molecules coordinating to the CUS via similar chemical functionalities, we expect pronounced differences, which are only slightly influenced by local static or dynamic disorder. Additionally, it should be possible to distinguish species directly coordinated to the CUS and physisorbed in the periphery of the framework. This helps determining and separating various active binding sites, as well as to derive preferred binding affinities to the CUS, leading to an improved understanding of host-guest interactions, in particular for competitive adsorption processes in the future.
Our analysis benefits from very fast magic angle spinning,37 which makes proton broadband decoupling unnecessary and enhances the sensitivity due to suppression of spinning sideband intensities. For the assignment of $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ MAS NMR spectra, we present two different but equivalent approaches. Either the signals might be assigned experimentally via a combination of spin-lattice relaxation and REDOR (rotational echo double resonance) experiments,37–39 or by state-of-the-art DFT cluster calculations of the NMR shifts that include not only all contributions to orbital, contact and pseudo-contact shifts, but also account for the magnetic couplings between the paramagnetic metal sites within the Curie-Weiss regime.40 While the NMR strategy is generally applicable as long as the materials remain paramagnetic, the theory for calculating hyperfine shifts in magnetically frustrated systems is new and still needs to be validated against experimental data. Both strategies avoid isotope labeling,41 which is usually expensive and not generally possible for all guests. Based on the assignment, the observed intensity modulations by the REDOR sequence provide information about the local reorientational disorder of guest molecules and framework.42–44
## Theoretical Background
The theoretical description of the nuclear shielding tensor is based, with one small modification, on a modern quantumchemical implementation45 of Kurland-McGarvey theory,46 which derives the pNMR shift tensor from EPR spin Hamiltonian parameters (g-, HFC-, and zero-field splitting - ZFS - Dtensors). The shielding tensor is then given as45,47–50
$$
\begin{array}{r}{{\bf{\sigma}}{{\bf{\sigma}}}^{I}={\bf{\sigma}}{\bf{\sigma}}{\bf{\sigma}}{\bf{{\sigma}}}_{\mathrm{{orb}}}-\frac{{{\mu}_{B}}}{\hbar{\gamma}_{I}k}\left\{\frac{1}{{\mathrm{T}}-\Theta}\right\}{\bf{g}}\cdot\left\langle{{\bf{{S}}}{\bf{{S}}}}\right\rangle\cdot{\bf{A}}^{I}}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\langle S S\rangle=\frac{\sum_{n m}Q_{m n}\langle n|\pmb{S}|m\rangle\langle m|\pmb{S}|n\rangle}{\sum_{n}\exp(-E_{n}/k T)}}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{Q_{m n}=\left\{\begin{array}{l l}{\exp\left(-\frac{E_{n}}{k T}\right),}&{E_{n}=E_{m}}\\ {\qquad}\\ {-\frac{k T}{E_{m}-E_{n}}\left[\exp\left(-\frac{E_{m}}{k T}\right)-\exp\left(-\frac{E_{n}}{k T}\right)\right],\ E_{n}\neq E_{m}}\end{array}\right.}\end{array}
$$
where $\pmb{\sigma}_{o r b}$ is the orbital shielding tensor, $\mu_{B}$ the Bohr magneton, $k$ the Boltzmann constant, $\hbar$ the reduced Planck constant, $\gamma_{I}$ the nuclear gyromagnetic ratio of nucleus $I.$ , T the absolute temperature, $\Theta$ the Weiss constant, $\mathbf{g}$ the electronic $\mathbf{g}$ -tensor of the system,  and $\mathbf{A}^{I}$ the hyperfine coupling tensor of nucleus $I.$ . 〈𝐒𝐒〉 is a spin dyadic with the components $\langle S_{\varepsilon}S_{\tau}\rangle$ evaluated in the manifold of eigenstates $|n\rangle$ , with the eigenenergies $E_{n}$ , of the ZFS Hamiltonian.45,48 Further details for Eq. 1, including the role of the Weiss constant in the temperature denominator, are given in the Supporting Information. From the shielding tensor, the isotropic shift is derived by Eq. 4
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\delta_{\mathrm{iso}}=~\sigma_{\mathrm{ref}}-\frac{1}{3}\mathrm{Tr}[\pmb{\sigma}^{\mathrm{I}}]\qquad,}\end{array}
$$
where $\upsigma_{\mathrm{ref}}$ is the isotropic shielding constant of a diamagnetic reference compound and $\mathrm{Tr}=\upsigma_{x x}+\sigma_{y y}+\upsigma_{z z}$ . $\delta_{\mathrm{iso}}$ includes the Ramsey-type orbital shift (orb), the Fermi-contact (FC) and the pseudo-contact (PC) contributions to the isotropic shift (Eq. 5)
$$
\delta_{\mathrm{iso}}=\delta_{\mathrm{orb}}+\delta_{\mathrm{FC}}+\delta_{\mathrm{PC}}
$$
The hyperfine interaction also leads to a shortening of spin-lattice $\left(\mathbf{T}_{1}\right)$ and spin-spin $\left(\mathbf{T}_{2}\right)$ relaxation times.31,36,51 The small $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ and the anisotropic part of the bulk magnetic susceptibility, which is not completely removed by magic-angle spinning, are the main reasons for signal broadening for paramagnetic compounds.31,52,53 The observed spin-lattice relaxation rates of nuclei in paramagnetic solids probed at high fields are usually dominated by the paramagnetic dipolar relaxation rate, since the Fermi contact contribution becomes negligible due to its dependence of the electronic Larmor frequency (Eq. S2). $T_{1}^{p a r a,d i p o l a r}$ depends on the distance r and on the electron-correlation time $\pmb{\tau_{\mathrm{s}}}$ (Eq. S3 and S8). Since $\tau_{\mathrm{s}}$ only depends on the transition between the electron Zeeman levels, it is considered to be constant for each metal ion independent from the coordinated ligands. The observed relaxation rate $1/\mathrm{T_{l}}^{\mathrm{para}}$ for a specific nucleus is then given by (Eq. 6),
$$
\frac{1}{T_{1}^{p a r a}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{T_{1}^{C r^{i}}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{c o n s t}{r_{M^{i}}^{6}}=c o n s t\cdot\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{r_{M^{i}}^{6}}=c o n s t\cdot R
$$
where $\mathtt{R}$ is the distance sum to all paramagnetic centers $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{i}}$ in its neighborhood. As a consequence, the ratio of the spin-lattice relaxation rates for the two resonances ref and $x$ depends only on the ratio of the distance sums R for the corresponding nuclei (Eq. 7). This will be used as a cost function to rank structure models derived from quantum-chemical calculations (a more detailed discussion is given in the Supporting Information).
$$
\begin{array}{r}{R_{x}=\frac{T_{1}^{r e f}}{T_{1}^{x}}\cdot R_{r e f}}\end{array}
$$
## Results and Discussion
## Characterization of X@Cr-MIL-101
Synthesis and activation of $\mathbf{H_{2}O}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{-M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}{-}101$ and DEA ${\mathcal{@}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}.$ MIL-101 were performed according to modified literature procedures starting from $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}\cdot9\:\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (bdc).54 Subsequently, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cr}$ -MIL-101 and DEA $@$ Cr-MIL-101 were grafted with 2-aminopyridine (2-AP) and 3-aminopyridine (3-AP) by equilibrating the activated MOFs with $0.25\mathrm{~M~}1{,}4$ -dioxane solutions. This concentration guarantees sufficiently high loadings to occupy all favored adsorption sites without completely filling the pores.
All samples have been characterized by powder $\mathrm{\Delta}X$ -ray diffraction, elemental analysis including atomic absorption spectroscopy, infrared spectroscopy, nitrogen physisorption and solution $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopy after digestion of the products. In all cases the integrity of the MIL-101 framework11,55 remains intact. The incorporation of DEA, 2-AP and 3-AP is demonstrated by the appearance of their characteristic stretching vibration bands.
The derived stoichiometries (Table 1) prove that water can be replaced by DEA, 2-AP and 3-AP. However, for DEA ${\mathcal{@}}\mathbf{Cr}.$ - MIL-101 an equivalent of 0.5 water molecules remain in the formula unit while an equivalent of 1.76 DEA molecules is adsorbed. While activated Cr-MIL-101 takes up 3.5 and 4.5 equivalents of 2-AP and 3-AP, respectively, the samples blocked with DEA adsorb significantly less 2-AP (1.8) and 3-AP (3.4). The amount of incorporated DEA remains constant even after exchanging with 2-AP and 3-AP. Interestingly, the total amount of adsorbed guest molecules $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ , DEA, 2-AP and 3-AP) exceeds the number of available CUS (two out of three $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ cations), indicating that for all samples both coordinated and noncoordinated guests are present. The reduction of the apparent BET surface areas, which is roughly halved from $\operatorname{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\ @\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}.$ MIL-101 $(\sim3000{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}\:\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ to $3{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{AP+DEA@Cr-MIL-101}$ $(\sim1687\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and the decrease of the pore sizes by ca. $0.5{\mathrm{nm}}$ seems to correlate with the amount of adsorbed guest molecules, which increases from DEA over 2-AP, 3-AP and $\mathrm{DEA}{+}2{-}\mathrm{AP}$ to $\mathrm{DEA}{+}3$ -AP (Table 1). Further details of the basic characterization are given in the Supporting Information.
## $^{13}\mathbf{C}$ MAS NMR spectroscopy of X@Cr-MIL-101 - Single adsorption of N-Donor compounds
The $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra of $\mathbf{H_{2}O@C r-M I L-}101$ , 2-AP@Cr-MIL-101, 3-AP@Cr-MIL-101 and DEA ${\mathcal{Q}}\mathrm{Cr}$ -MIL101 (Figure 1, right column) exhibit resonances in the typical diamagnetic shift region $\left(0{-}180\mathrm{ppm}\right)$ . In addition, hyperfine shifts are observed with positive $(180-500\mathrm{ppm})$ and negative $(0{-}380\ \mathrm{ppm})$ signs. For each isotropic resonance, a maximum of two weak spinning sidebands is observed at a spinning speed of $40\mathrm{kHz}$ , indicating that the magnitude of the anisotropy of the hyperfine shift is below $250\ \mathrm{ppm}$ for all observed resonances. Similarly, the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ anisotropic hyperfine shifts were estimated to $200-240\mathrm{ppm}$ (Table S6). Moderate anisotropies suggest a significant flexibility of the bdc units and the adsorbed guest molecules. The local dynamics is one reason for the relatively small anisotropic hyperfine interaction and narrow signals. Combined with the short spin-lattice times, which allow for very fast sampling, and ultrafast magic angle spinning, spectra with an excellent resolution could be obtained in a reasonable period of time.
Nevertheless, since the classic correlations between shift and chemical fragment are established for diamagnetic compounds only, additional information is needed to assign the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ MAS spectra. Here we present a strategy based on the combined use of distance relations derived from spin-lattice relaxation experiments38 and spectral editing relying on a heteronuclear $^{13}\mathrm{{C}^{-1}\mathrm{{H}}}$ dipolar dephasing induced with a REDOR experiment.37,39 While most of the resonances can already be assigned by matching experimental distance sums $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{x}}$ derived from the $\mathrm{T}_{1}$ ratios (Eq. 7) to the ones $({\bf R}_{\mathrm{DFT}})$ calculated from characteristic structural fragments, the ${}^{13}{\bf C}\{{}^{1}{\bf H}\}$ REDOR data provide complementary information by discriminating between resonances according to the numbers of protons attached to the carbon atoms responsible for the targeted signal.
Even more importantly, deviations between the experimentally observed and expected dephasing values for rigid C, CH, $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ units are unequivocally caused by dynamic reorientational processes since the strength of the dipolar coupling between a carbon atom and its covalently bonded protons is well known and relatively independent of the local structural properties. Reductions of the expected CH and $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ coupling constants have been frequently used to identify motional processes in the past.42–44 Such analyses provide similar results compared to $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR studies in the fast motion limit, and avoid chemical labeling. We make use of this within our strategy to get insight not only into the preferred adsorption sites and their structural build-up, but also to derive a reasonable picture of the local dynamics at the adsorption sites.
For $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cr}$ -MIL-101, 2-AP $@$ Cr-MIL-101, 3-AP@Cr-MIL101 and DEA ${\mathcal{Q}}\mathrm{Cr}$ -MIL-101, we extracted structural fragments (Figures 1 and S8) from the framework, large enough to allow for the calculation of the distance sums ${\bf R}_{\mathrm{DFT}}$ (Eq. 6) between the ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ cations of the IBU and the carbon atoms of both the bdc linkers and the adsorbed molecules, but still small enough to allow for fast structure optimizations at DFT level. The adsorbate molecules were placed at the CUS of the IBU with their Lewis basic centers bonded to the Lewis acidic $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ ions and relaxed freely. The calculated distance sum ${\bf R}_{\mathrm{DFT}}$ of one carbon atom was chosen as reference $R_{D F T}^{r e f}$ . All the other ${\bf R}_{\mathrm{DFT}}$ values for the other carbon atoms were then compared to the experimental ones $(\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{x}})$ derived from the ratio of the spin-lattice relaxation times for the reference signal $T_{1}^{r e f}$ and the targeted resonance $T_{1}^{x}$ as given in Eq. 7.
For the reference, we chose the signal at $-350~\mathrm{ppm}$ , since it is well separated from any other observed resonance and is present in all four compounds. It exhibits a very small intensity reduction in the $^{13}{\bf C}\{^{1}{\mathrm{H}}\}$ REDOR spectra well below $10\%$ (Figures 1 middle column and S9) and thus originates from the quaternary carbon atoms 1, 8 or 2, 7 of the bdc linkers. When assigning the atoms 1, 8 to the reference signal at $-350\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , $R_{D F T}^{r e f}$ calculates to $30.3{\bullet}10^{-4}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-6}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cr}$ -MIL-101. Using Eq. 7, $\mathtt{R_{x}}$ for the second observed signal at $130\mathrm{ppm}$ is calculated based on $\mathrm{T}_{1}$ (Table S5) to $17.4{\bullet}10^{-4}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-6}$ , which is one order of magnitude larger compared to the ${\bf R}_{\mathrm{DFT}}$ values for carbon atoms $\mathbf{3-6}$ and 2, 7 (Figure 1 middle column). In contrast, when assigning carbon atoms 2, 7 to the reference signal $(R_{D F T}^{r e f}=3.66^{\bullet10^{-4}}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-}$ 6), $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{x}}(130\mathrm{ppm})$ is $2.10{\cdot}10^{-4}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-6}$ , which matches the expectation of $\mathrm{\bfR}_{\mathrm{DFT}}=1.80\bullet10^{-4}\ \mathring\mathrm{A}^{-6}$ for the aromatic CH units of the bdc linkers. This assignment is furthermore, supported by the stronger intensity reduction within the ${}^{13}{\bf C}\{{}^{1}{\mathrm{H}}\}$ REDOR spectra, suggesting proton bearing carbon atoms.
The assignment for 2-AP@Cr-MIL-101, 3-AP@Cr-MIL-101 and DEA $@$ Cr-MIL-101 was done in a similar way and the result is summarized in Figure 1. Additionally, a full list of spinlattice relaxation times and experimental distance sums is given in Table S5 together with the calculated ${\bf R}_{\mathrm{DFT}}$ values obtained from the model fragments. The general agreement between $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{exp}}$ and ${\bf R}_{\mathrm{DFT}}$ is very good with the exception of the carbon atoms closest to the paramagnetic centers. Examples are the ortho-carbon atoms 9 and 13 of 2-AP as well as 14 and 18 of 3-AP. With the exception of meta carbon atoms 15 and 17 for $3{\cdot}\mathrm{AP}@\mathrm{Cr}.$ MIL-101 the $\mathrm{T}_{1}$ data are decisive enough to assign all resonances. However, for the latter case the intensity modulation of the $^{13}{\bf C}\{^{1}\mathrm{H}\}$ REDOR spectra allowed to distinguish between the $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and C-H units unambiguously.
For DEA two different adsorbate species could be distinguished. While the distance sums for the resonances at $300\mathrm{ppm}$ and $-46\mathrm{\ppm}$ hint to DEA molecules $(\mathbf{19}^{\circ},2\mathbf{0}^{\circ})$ coordinated to the CUS, the significantly smaller R values for the signals 19 and 20 at the typical diamagnetic shifts of DEA indicate additional physisorbed DEA species in a second coordination sphere. Taking into account the results of the chemical analysis (Table 1), the CUS are probably occupied by the remaining 0.5 water molecules and only 1.5 equivalents are saturated with DEA molecules. This leaves an equivalent of residual 0.26 DEA molecules uncoordinated but still close to the IBU.
In particular, for 3-AP, DEA and the bdc linkers, the intensity reduction of the signals for the CH and $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ units within the $^{13}{\bf C}\{^{1}{\mathrm{H}}\}$ REDOR spectra is considerably less pronounced as expected for rigid $\mathrm{CH_{x}}$ fragments (Figure 1, middle column). The predicted dephasing values were derived using a diamagnetic model system by simulating the intensity reductions for a dephasing time $\tau_{\mathrm{deph}}$ as short as one rotor period based on the spin-density matrix59 approach. The effect of the hyperfine interaction during $\tau_{\mathrm{deph}}$ and the hard $180^{\circ}$ impulses on the carbon13 and proton channels were modeled by treating the anisotropic part of the hyperfine interaction as chemical shift anisotropy. The corresponding coupling constants $\delta_{\mathrm{hyper}}$ for $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 1 right column) and $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ (Figure S10) were estimated based on spinning side band intensities of the MAS spectra (a more detailed description is given in the Supporting Information).
For the presented analysis, we used a REDOR sequence with conventional $180^{\circ}$ impulses, instead of more advanced recoupling and polarization transfer schemes60–62 relying on adiabatic pulses. On the one hand, due to the inherent flexibility of the $\mathbf{X}@\mathbf{Cr-MIL-}101$ compounds the observed $\delta_{\mathrm{hyper}}$ are relatively small, allowing for a sufficiently homogenous excitation with short and hard $180^{\circ}$ impulses. On the other hand, adiabatic $180^{\circ}$ pulses are inevitably on the same order as $\tau_{\mathrm{deph}}$ when using ultrafast MAS to match the adiabacicity condition, which makes simulations more demanding and might complicate the extraction of dynamical information from the observed intensity reductions. A detailed description of the model system and the simulations is given in the Supporting Information. Summarizing, without dynamic disorder, we expect intensity reductions of well below $10\%$ for C and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ units and around $65\%$ as well as $80\%$ for CH and $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ units, respectively, for the experimentally used conditions of the $^{13}{\bf C}\{^{1}{\mathrm{H}}\}$ REDOR experiments (Table S7). Please note, that we allow for a variation of roughly $10\%$ for the expected intensity reductions to account for structural changes for the targeted materials and finite size effects within the simulations.
Large deviations between the observed and simulated intensity reductions must be caused by fast large-angle reorientations of guest molecules and bdc linkers, since they are the only motional processes, which influence the dephasing enough on the order of a few tens of $\upmu\mathrm{s}$ . To keep the models as simple as possible, we used simple reorientational jump models adapted to the local environment and symmetry to calculate the reduced the dipolar coupling constants for the C-H bonds of CH and $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ units, following strategies given in refs. 37,39. The coupling constants were then fed back into the REDOR simulations to derive the dephasing under the influence of motion. We observed that only specific jump models match the observed and calculated dephasing values (Figure 2)
The total computed $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ shifts given in Table 2 and S8 do thus reflect particularly the FC contribution and therefore the delocalization of spin density over the ligand framework, as well as to some extent spin polarization effects. Sign and magnitude of the computed $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ (Table 2 and S8) and $^1\mathrm{H}$ (Table S9) hyperfine shifts agree well with experiment, and the computed data have been helpful in the signal assignment. Notably, the reduction of hyperfine shifts due to the Curie-Weiss regime by a factor $\textstyle{\frac{\mathrm{~T~}}{\mathrm{~T-\Theta~}}}=0.761$ for $\mathbf{H_{2}O}@\mathbf{Cr-MIL-}101$ brings computed and experimental shifts into significantly better agreement. We used the experimentally determined reduction factor for all $X@\mathbf{Cr-}$ MIL-101 derivates, since we do not expect large differences for their magnetic susceptibilities compared to the one of $\operatorname{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\ @\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}-$ MIL-101. This assumption is based on the magnetic properties for molecular compounds with isolated $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ clusters with $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and pyridine based ligands.57,58,71 The corresponding exchange constants are similar to the one observed for $\mathbf{H_{2}O@C r-M I L-}101$ and vary only between 10 and $13~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Additionally, for a simulation temperature of $325\mathrm{~K~}$ , a change of $\Theta$ by $20~\mathrm{K}$ only affects the hyperfine shifts by less than $5\%$ . We note, that the negative deviations of the $\mathbf{g}$ -tensor from the free-electron value only reduce the computed shifts additionally by about $1\%$ .
The shifts may be rationalized to a great extent by the spin-density plots for the cluster models (Figure 3). For $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cr}$ -MIL101, $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ nuclei 1-8 split into two sets: carbons 2 and 7 have negative spin densities, the others exhibit positive spin densities. ${2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{AP}@\mathrm{Cr-MIL}\mathrm{-}101}$ and 3-AP@Cr-MIL-101 exhibit additionally alternating positive and negative spin densities within the aminopyridine ring. For carbon atoms 9-18 we may distinguish negative spin densities for atoms 10, 12, 15, 17, positive spin densities for the remaining atoms. For DEA ${\mathcal{Q}}\mathrm{Cr}$ -MIL-101, carbons 19' and 20' for the DEA molecules attached to the CUS have positive and negative spin densities, respectively, while the non-coordinated ones (carbons 19 and 20) exhibit the diamagnetic shifts only.
In all cases, the sign of the assigned hyperfine shifts (see Table 2) agrees perfectly with these spin-density analyses. We note that the extremely large positive spin densities at the carboxyl carbon atoms 1 and 8 in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}@$ Cr-MIL-101 (Figure 3a) give rise to very large positive computed shifts at around $+1683\mathrm{ppm}$ . However, the large positive $\pi$ -spin density also causes very short transverse $\left(\mathrm{T}_{2}\right)$ relaxation times that result in severe signal broadening. As a consequence, the signal disappears in the noise level. In all cases, negative spin densities, and thus negative hyperfine shifts are due to $\pi$ -σ-spin polarization mechanisms. Positive spin-delocalization and negative spin-polarization contributions may also partly compensate. Carbon atoms of the linkers 3-6 in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cr}$ -MIL-101 are examples of such a cancellation (Figure 3a), giving rise to signals near the positions expected for such nuclei in diamagnetic samples (Table 2).10
## $^{13}\mathbf{C}$ MAS NMR spectroscopy of X@Cr-MIL-101 - Competitive adsorption of N-Donor compounds
Based on the results obtained for $\mathbf{X}@\mathbf{Cr-MIL-}101$ with X being either $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , 2-AP, 3-AP or DEA, it is now possible to analyze competition experiments with two components. If 3-AP is coadsorbed with 2-AP and DEA, respectively, only the characteristic hyperfine-shifted $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR signals (Figure 4) for 3-AP are observed (14 – 18) while for the other components only the typical diamagnetic shifts were found. In contrast, for adsorbate mixtures of 2-AP and DEA both guest molecules show hyperfine-shifted and diamagnetic signals. The most pronounced hyperfine-shifted ones are 11 and 10 of 2-AP and $\mathbf{19^{\prime}}$ as well as 20' of DEA, representative for ligands coordinated to the CUS. Notably, the peaks for coordinated 2-AP are more intense compared to bonded DEA. Consequently, we conclude that the binding strength at the CUS increases in the order $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}<$ ${\mathrm{DEA}}\approx2{\cdot}\mathrm{AP}<3.$ -AP. This is somewhat surprising as it opposes the chemical intuition that the strongest base 2-AP should exhibit the highest binding affinity. Since the computed binding energies are with $142.6\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ (2-AP) and $143.4\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ (3-AP) very similar, we attribute this to the steric hindrance of the amino group in ortho position. The markedly higher mobility of 3-AP (Figure 2) leads to higher entropic contributions to the equilibrium and thus to a stronger binding affinity.
Nevertheless, a similar trend was observed for the larger adsorption band for the d-d transitions of ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ around $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ in the UV-VIS spectra of the $\mathbf{X}@\mathbf{Cr-MIL-}101$ derivates (Figure S19). DEA, 2-AP and 3-AP give rise to increasing blue-shifts with respect to $\mathbf{H_{2}O@C r-M I L-}101$ , indicating the strongest interaction between ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ and 3-AP, while DEA and 2-AP exhibit similar and smaller shifts, respectively. In contrast to the NMR data, which allowed us to distinguish between guest molecules coordinated at the CUS and those physisorbed in a second coordination sphere, the UV-VIS spectra are not sufficiently discriminating to answer this question (Figure S19).
The trend of the binding strength seems to be reflected in the magnitude of the hyperfine shifts, if atoms with the same number of bonds away from the paramagnetic center are compared. For example, the hyperfine shifts $(\delta_{\mathrm{FC}})$ of the signals for 19' (DEA), 9 and 13 (2-AP) as well as for 14 and 18 (3-AP) increase from $248~\mathrm{ppm}$ to $324~\mathrm{ppm}$ (Table 2). However, the same values scatter markedly within the quantum-chemical calculations (Table 2), which indicates that the hyperfine interaction depends not only on the binding strength but also on the structure and the dynamics of the complex and thus on computational aspects such as model construction and method used for structure optimization.
## Conclusion
$\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cr}$ -MIL-101 was successfully synthesized and loaded with 2-AP, 3-AP and DEA as well as binary mixtures of these ligands. The well resolved $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ MAS NMR spectra of the individual Cr-MIL-101 derivates feature both hyperfine shifted signals and resonances within the typical shift region of the diamagnetic guest molecules. By combining distance relations derived from spin-lattice relaxation data and structural fragments with REDOR recoupling experiments to group the carbon atoms into C or $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ and CH or $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ moieties, we were able to unambiguously assign all these resonances. While the hyperfine shifted signals are characteristic for guest molecules coordinated to the CUS, the other resonances represent unspecifically physisorbed guests in a second coordination sphere. In particular, the hyperfine shifted signals present a clear signature for each guest, and thus allow to decide which of the guest molecules is coordinated to the CUS even for competition experiments using binary mixtures. The derived binding preference depicts the following order: $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}<\mathrm{DEA}\approx2{\cdot}\mathrm{AP}<3{\cdot}\mathrm{AP}$ .
The experimental assignment has been used to evaluate a computational approach, which amends a modern implementation of the Kurland/McGarvey theory for chemical shifts of systems with arbitrary spin multiplicity by a scaling to the Curie-Weiss regime of magnetically coupled metal centers above the transition temperature. The computed hyperfine shifts are dominated by the Fermi-contact term, which in turn accurately reflects the computed spin-density distributions within the clusters. The Curie-Weiss scaling brings the computed shifts to within about $+1-20\%$ of the experimental values, also allowing shifts to be predicted in cases where paramagnetic line broadening did not allow detection. This demonstrates the potential of the modified cluster approach to obtain accurate chemical shifts also for exchange-coupled systems like the present MOFs.
Our results show that both the experimental and computational approaches present equivalent assignment strategies and might be used independently in the future. Nevertheless, the NMR experiments offer additional information about the dynamical disorder of the guest molecules and the framework as the intensity modulations for the $^{13}{\bf C}\{^{1}{\mathrm{H}}\}$ REDOR spectra of proton bearing carbon atoms are sensitive to reorientations of the CH bonds. While the bdc linkers as well as the 3-AP and DEA molecules perform fast thermally activated large angle jumps combined with toggling motions, 2-AP molecules coordinated to the CUS are largely immobile. We attribute this to the considerable steric hindrance of the amino function in ortho-position with the IBU. Our study shows, that for the Cr-MIL-101 derivates, solid-state $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectroscopy is well suited to identify the coordinating species as well as to determine their relative interaction strengths, as long as an unambiguous assignment can be achieved. Cr-MIL-101 is, however, only one example among the prominent class of MOFs featuring coordinatively unsaturated sites with paramagnetic metal ions including HKUST, CPO-27 and MIL-100, which were discussed for probing or recovering small gas molecules like CO, $^{72}\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{73}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\ 74}$ as well as for catalysis. All these applications crucially rely on the interaction between the gas molecules and the preferred adsorption sites. The presented strategy has the potential to provide a better understanding for the nature of such host-guest interactions, since it allows to unveil guest-specific coordination at the CUS, separate them from physisorbed guests and provide a microscopic picture of the adsorption mechanisms.
## Experimental section
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) experiments were carried out in Bragg-Brentano geometry on a Panalytical X'pert Pro diffractometer equipped with a X'Celerator Scientific RTMS detector using Ni filtered $\mathrm{{Cu}\times\mathrm{{a}}}$ radiation $(\lambda=1.54187\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $40\ensuremath{\mathrm{kV}}$ , $40\ \mathrm{mA}$ ). Measurements were made in the range of $2^{\circ}-30^{\circ}$ (2θ) with a step size of $0.017^{\circ}$ . The theoretical PXRD pattern was simulated from an optimized structure (using the Universal forcefield of the Forcite tool implemented in Materials Studio 5.0) obtained from a crystallographic information file.75 The pattern was simulated with $\mathrm{{Cu}_{\mathrm{{Ka}}}}$ radiation, reflection geometry and using the Pearson profile functions. The half width of the Bragg reflections was set to $0.15^{\circ}$ . The parameters of the cubic cell of the space group $F d\bar{3}m$ (No. 227) were set to $87.5\mathrm{nm}$ .
Attenuated total reflectance IR spectra were recorded in the range $400-4000{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ with a resolution of $4\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ on a Jasco FT/IR-6100 spectrometer with a PIKEGladi ATR accessory.
The samples for chemical analyses were prepared in the glove box under argon atmosphere after evacuating at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ . Carbon, hydrogen and nitrogen contents (wt $\%$ ) were obtained on a Vario elementar EL III. Chromium contents (wt $\%$ ) were determined by atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS) in extinction mode on a Varian AA100 using a $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}/$ acetylene flame upon chemical digestion. EDX spectroscopy was carried out on a Joel JSM 6400 scanning electron microscope equipped with a Noran energy dispersive X-ray analyzer and using a beam voltage of $20\mathrm{kV}$ .
Nitrogen sorption measurements were performed on a Quantachrome Nova 2000e at $77\mathrm{K}$ after evacuating the adsorbents at $363~\mathrm{K}$ for $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{h}}$ under reduced pressure. Brunauer-Emmet-Teller (BET) equivalent surface areas were determined in the relative pressure range between 0.06 and 0.15 to accommodate for microporous materials.76 Specific total pore volumes were determined at a relative pressure of 0.98 according to the Gurvich rule.77 The pore-size distributions were derived using the nitrogen NLDFT model at $77\mathrm{K}$ for the adsorption branch assuming a silica/zeolite surface and a spherical/cylindrical pore shape which is implemented in the Quantachrome ASiQ $\mathbf{v}3.0$ software package.
Dissolving the frameworks in a ${\mathrm{NaOD/D_{2}O}}$ solution of $\mathrm{pH}=8.5$ and precipitating the paramagnetic $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{OD})_{3}$ allowed us to record well resolved solution $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra and to quantify the modification degrees of both the linkers and IBUs by comparing the signal areas of products with that of the educts.
Diffuse reflectance UV/VIS absorption spectra were recorded on an Agilent Cary 300 Scan spectrometer in the range from $190\mathrm{nm}$ to $800\mathrm{nm}$ with scan speeds of $0.5\mathrm{nm/sec}$ .
The magnetic susceptibility data of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O-C r-M I L-101}$ were collected using a MPMSXL-5 SQUID magnetometer under an applied field of $1591540\ \mathrm{A/m}$ over the temperature range $50~\mathrm{K}-400~\mathrm{K}$ . The solid sample was prepared in a gelatin capsule. The agreement factor R between the experimental and simulated data was calculated as $\mathrm{R}=\Sigma_{\mathrm{i}}(\chi_{\mathrm{Mi}}\mathrm{^{exp}-\chi_{\mathrm{Mi}}\mathrm{^{sim})^{2}/\Sigma_{\mathrm{i}}(\chi_{\mathrm{Mi}}\mathrm{^{sim})^{2}}}}$ .
The EPR spectrum was recorded at $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -band frequencies ${(9.4~\mathrm{GHz})}$ by sweeping the external magnetic field from 0.1 to $0.6\mathrm{T}$ . A modulation frequency $100\mathrm{kHz}$ and an amplitude of $0.2\mathrm{mT}$ was used. The conversion time was set to $40.96~\mathrm{{ms}}$ .
Solid-state rotor-synchronized Hahn-echo pulse sequences (τ duration of one rotor period: $25~{\upmu\mathrm{s}},$ ) under MAS rates of $40\mathrm{kHz}$ at 9.4 T using a $1.9\mathrm{mm}$ triple-resonance probe afforded well resolved $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra by suppression of background signals, reducing the number and intensities of spinning sidebands and suppressed ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}.$ - $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ dipolar couplings without rf-pulses. The $90^{\circ}$ pulse length for $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ was $2.0\upmu\mathrm{s}$ . The recycle delays were $50~\mathrm{ms}$ . The averaged temperature in the rotor at $\mathrm{v_{rot}=40\ k H z}$ was determined to $325\mathrm{K}$ by referencing with $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ .78 The spin-lattice relaxation times $\mathrm{T}_{1}$ were obtained with the inversion recovery method with a recycle delay of $0.3\mathrm{~s~}$ and time increments ranging from $0.5~\mathrm{ms}$ to $256~\mathrm{ms}$ . The $^{13}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ REDOR type recoupling experiments were performed with two $180^{\circ}$ pulses on the $^1\mathrm{H}$ channel at the center of each of the two rotation periods $(\tau_{\mathrm{deph}}=50~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ ) while a $180^{\circ}$ pulse was applied on the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ at the center of the two rotation periods. $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ and $^{13}\bar{\mathbf{C}}^{\ 180^{\circ}}$ pulse lengths are $2.8~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ and $4~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ , respectively.
DFT computations of the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ shifts were done at structures of the four model clusters optimized at PBE0-D3/def2-TZVP level,79–83 using the Turbomole program.84 Subsequently, the magnetic-resonance parameters have been computed using appropriate $9\mathrm{s}7\mathrm{p}4\mathrm{d}$ metal85 and IGLO-II main-group element86 basis sets. In these calculations, a ferromagnetically coupled spin arrangement within the cluster has been chosen. The HFC and $\mathrm{^g}$ -tensor calculations with the ORCA code87 used a modified PBE40 hybrid functional (PBE0 with increased $40\%$ exact-exchange admixture) that we recently found to perform well for solid-state pNMR shift calculations,70 and which is also known to give excellent hyperfine couplings and $\mathbf{g}$ -tensors in relativistic computations for molecules.88 The computed HFCs have been normalized to the number of spin centers present.89 Zero-field splitting (ZFS) tensors were computed using the PBE exchange-correlation functional,90 and the Pederson-Khanna second-order perturbation approach91 with van Wüllen’s prefactors.92 For both $\mathrm{^g}$ -tensors and ZFS, the necessary spin-orbit matrix elements were computed within the spin-orbit mean-field approximation $(\mathrm{SOMF})^{93}$ implemented in ORCA. Orbital shieldings were obtained with the PBE40 functional using Gaussian09 (for both open- and closed-shell cases).94 All pNMR computations have been performed for $\mathrm{T}=325\ \mathrm{K}$ . The value of the Weiss constant $\left(\Theta=-102\mathrm{K}\right)$ ) has been taken from experiment (Figure S7). Both $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ shifts were referenced to tetramethylsilane (TMS) at the same level (using a PBE/def2-TZVP structure for TMS; the values are $\sigma_{r e f}^{C}=189.23$ ppm and $\sigma_{r e f}^{H}=31.68\mathrm{ppm})$ .
